An $n times n$ matrix $matbold(A)$ with $n$ distinct eigenvalues is diagonalisable: the corresponding $n$ eigenvectors are linearly independent, hence form a basis of $RR^n$ or $CC^n$. This criterion is sufficient but not necessary.

Necessary and sufficient: for every eigenvalue $lambda$,

$ M_lambda = m_lambda. $

Indeed, taking bases $B_lambda$ of the eigenspaces $E_lambda$ of the distinct eigenvalues $lambda_1, ..., lambda_r$, the union $B_(lambda_1) union dots.v union B_(lambda_r)$ is linearly independent with

$ sum_(i = 1)^r m_(lambda_i) = sum_(i = 1)^r M_(lambda_i) = n, $

so it is a basis of $RR^n$ or $CC^n$ exactly when no defect remains.
